#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178e8522-f6d6-5615-a7db-e649a18d2cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304275} {:answer/id #uuid "af616c88-26d1-5b72-b19c-57726efe8a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304276} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5c5dbf-cfb2-5f71-809b-221068a282fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304277} {:answer/id #uuid "7786dcac-bd13-5ba2-a4d8-028bb77023e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304278} {:answer/id #uuid "6230f0e6-d0a4-5251-bc20-ccbe2b374168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178e8522-f6d6-5615-a7db-e649a18d2cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304275} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3231972-1caa-5cea-86d8-e37996473f0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bc95deb-40f2-508a-aa44-de7fe1d1479e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141092" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "731f18bd-d7d6-51a1-b655-6def4699992a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304282} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4284e2-6405-5af1-8e18-6ed7e5f0ceb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304283} {:answer/id #uuid "c7868473-ef33-540e-afff-2111b32510ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304284} {:answer/id #uuid "e80c9a44-9797-521d-8a6b-d9408f81ba46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304285} {:answer/id #uuid "00936aaf-aa47-5f4c-8b88-98068c612fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e80c9a44-9797-521d-8a6b-d9408f81ba46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304285} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a69b8c-cfd3-597e-be8d-e19a8a9f044c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7f17189-7b82-5218-afd5-ddec775b868c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141107" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,y(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{x}^{4}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{x}^{4}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{4} \\\\ -1 & =A(-1)^{4} \\\\ -1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=(-1){x}^{4}=-{x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cf05be1-4507-5c1a-8a3c-31ab08cc9d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304289} {:answer/id #uuid "3c94db7b-8874-5d6d-b048-2fd3ff11f860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304290} {:answer/id #uuid "191114dc-6acc-568b-9cc5-d71bfeca996b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304291} {:answer/id #uuid "6632709a-3d7c-5ba7-a42f-db9b5d4aec6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304292} {:answer/id #uuid "a005a8c3-1eb7-5608-bf28-850aabbb8a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191114dc-6acc-568b-9cc5-d71bfeca996b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304291} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cab405-7421-5b6f-a5c2-a8b4adeaf231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea6ff592-81a4-59f3-84d6-c97bdc2e3a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141116" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A+0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283c827a-e42f-5a0a-a767-1f40d3564e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "283c827a-e42f-5a0a-a767-1f40d3564e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304296} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7462996-4474-52b4-8b55-d9744d03b6e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ad1b80f-f6b4-5a82-9600-dba1f5e03f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352660" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{4y}{x},\\,y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{{2}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{16} \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278ca932-6bd2-5409-a12c-59e55f3a91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304299} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb93bf5-9543-57f8-bc26-332e5dccb902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304300} {:answer/id #uuid "e93a9665-2338-5665-a5fb-0695e3b625bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304301} {:answer/id #uuid "f246ebc7-45e4-5632-8c1c-03c9ed1a380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304302} {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d3c876-9a73-5d7f-be91-1b4288459661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df42d33c-ab0b-52f4-82a5-1108ad30f682" :question/math-academy-id "q-79330" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={e}^{-4x}$\n- $y={e}^{4x}$\n- $y=4{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{-4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 8{e}^{-4x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{4x} & =4{e}^{4x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={e}^{4x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4(4{e}^{x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}16{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 12{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
